00:00:06.747 --> 00:00:07.830
MIKE FISHER: Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:08.530
I'm Mike Fisher.

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:09.640
I'm the CTO at Etsy.

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.140
Thanks for joining me today.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.380
So I've been at
Etsy about a year.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.800
Before that, I ran a
consultancy for about 10 years,

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.590
and one of my clients
happened to be Etsy.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:23.030
So I've known them
for a very long time.

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:25.960
And I'm excited to share
the story about Etsy

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:29.530
and move into the
cloud with you today.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:32.180
So Etsy is a global marketplace.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.570
If you're not
familiar with us, we

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:37.180
have two million
creative entrepreneurs

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:41.320
that are connected to over
35 million active buyers

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.900
through 50 million unique
items that they make.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.480
And these are artisans
and crafts people

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.480
who hand-make items or
collected vintage items

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:52.510
and sell them to the buyers.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.340
We see ourselves
in a marketplace

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.230
as a force for good.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:00.680
In the US, where only 40%
of entrepreneurs are women,

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:04.739
the creative entrepreneurs
at Etsy are over 87%.

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:06.530
Most of them work from
home because they're

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:07.720
artists and crafts people.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.660
They turn their extra bedroom,
their kitchen table, the garage

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:14.100
into their studios
and workplaces

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.820
And our sellers are really the
hearts of Etsy and the products

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.290
that they make.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:23.020
And I'm going to share with
you a quick video about one

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:26.530
of our sellers,
Dianne Nordt, who

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:29.850
I find very inspiring
about the care

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:33.641
and what she puts into
making Etsy's products.

00:01:33.641 --> 00:01:36.587
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:01:36.587 --> 00:01:41.497
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:43.952
[BARKING]

00:01:44.934 --> 00:01:46.898
[BAAING]

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:31.324
[END PLAYBACK]

00:03:31.324 --> 00:03:32.990
MIKE FISHER: So
hopefully, that gave you

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:36.140
a sense of the type of
care that our sellers put

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:39.840
into their products and how
much that really means to them.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.080
So that's what we find the
power of the marketplace

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.755
is to be able to enable
this creed of entrepreneurs.

00:03:44.755 --> 00:03:47.420
But we not only see
the power that we

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:51.620
can do good for our sellers
but also for our own employees.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:56.000
Again, in a world
where we only see

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.860
14% women engineers in the
industry, Etsy is a leader.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:04.640
We see over 30% of our
engineers are women.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.970
And if you increase that for
the entire technology team,

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:09.860
it's much higher than that.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.030
Five out of eight of our
executive team are women.

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.075
And we have gender
parity across our Board.

00:04:14.075 --> 00:04:16.430
And so not only do
we see ourselves

00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.470
as a force for
good in the world,

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:20.839
but we also think that we can
do good for our employees.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:27.460
Last fall, we announced our
mission of Keep Commerce Human.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:31.170
And a lot of companies
try to use technology

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:32.160
to replace people.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.190
But as Etsy, we see the
mission as using technology

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:38.820
to enable people to
connect with each other

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:44.120
and to augment that relationship
and their task that they do.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:47.550
And whether that's connecting
a seller to a buyer,

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:49.830
and trying to
eliminate the currency

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:51.450
issues or the
communication issues,

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.880
or the shipping Issues
across the world,

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.290
or trying to empower an
employee to help fight fraud,

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:01.440
we see technology as augmenting
this instead of replacing.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.600
And in a world of increasing
automation and commoditization,

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:08.290
creativity can't be automated.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:13.590
And that human connection
really cannot be commoditized.

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:15.900
This is what drew me
to Etsy is the ability

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.670
to use two things
that I love, which

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:22.010
is helping support entrepreneurs
and applying technology.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:24.690
And it's the combination
of those two things

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.490
that brought me to
Etsy and keep all

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:28.740
of us motivated each
day to continue working

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.180
for the sellers and buyers.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.800
Now, Etsy engineers,
as I mentioned,

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:38.260
I've known them for
a number of years.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.080
They've always had a really
strong engineering culture.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:45.400
And we were very innovative
in the early years in some

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:48.220
of the areas like CI/CD.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:49.810
So we were one of
the early places

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:52.450
that did continuous integration,
conduced employment.

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:55.450
DevOps, the concept of
monitoring everything--

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:57.820
all of these were
early, early concepts

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:00.130
that Etsy participated in.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:03.160
And at that time, there
weren't a lot of tools.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:05.140
And so they developed them.

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:06.640
The analogy would
be that if we're

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:09.820
building our house, that we
actually went out and cut down

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:14.080
the trees, and hand you the
logs, and made our own floors,

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:16.000
like the pioneers.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.880
And when I came on
board a year ago,

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:22.540
one of the first tasks that our
CEO asked me to look at was,

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.400
should we be in some
of these areas again?

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.487
Should we be hand-building
our own monitoring stocks?

00:06:28.487 --> 00:06:30.820
And one of the things that I
looked at first, because it

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:34.030
was such a big part
of our organization,

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:35.410
was the infrastructure.

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:38.110
So we were still
in data centers,

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:40.320
a lot of cases still
running on bare metal.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.452
And so I took a look
at that and said,

00:06:42.452 --> 00:06:44.410
is this really where we
should be concentrating

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:46.820
our engineering efforts
or are we in a world

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:51.360
now that we can leverage
a partner to do this with?

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:54.520
And so this started us down
the cloud path to look at--

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:57.370
can we find a vendor who
could partner with us,

00:06:57.370 --> 00:07:00.640
and support us, and let
us concentrate on helping

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:02.440
the sellers and buyers?

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:06.310
And so continuing that analogy
of the house, we're in a world

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:09.580
now where you can hire an
architect and subcontractors

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:11.460
and stuff that build
that house for us.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:14.200
And so this is what we
looked at the cloud journey

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:17.380
about a year ago and said,
let's start down that path

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:19.290
and let's figure out
who we can partner with.

00:07:23.026 --> 00:07:27.080
Now, a lot of the things that
make Etsy unique and special

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:30.020
also make it incredibly
challenging from a technology

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:30.930
perspective.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:36.740
So all of those 50 million items
are unique, handmade, creative.

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:39.800
We like to think of it sometimes
as the world's largest flash

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.740
sale, in that once
that item is sold,

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:46.080
there's often not
another item like it.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.600
We also have unstructured data.

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:51.740
So we don't have a structured
listing, we don't have SKUs,

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:53.790
we don't have catalogs
and things like that

00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:55.340
that a lot of our
products fall into,

00:07:55.340 --> 00:07:58.470
that a typical e-commerce
company would have.

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:00.350
And so because of
that, it makes a lot

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:03.080
of things challenging
in terms of finding

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:06.170
that right item for those
buyers and to search

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:07.730
all 50 million items.

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:09.230
So lots of challenges.

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:11.030
And of course, we're
a global company.

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:13.100
We're in almost every
country in the world.

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:15.740
And so connecting the
sellers across that

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:18.050
to sell their products,
to communicate,

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:21.440
to transfer currencies, to ship
those products-- all of those

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:23.719
make our jobs really,
really challenging.

00:08:23.719 --> 00:08:25.010
But that's what's fun about it.

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:31.410
One of the areas that is
specifically challenging

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:34.530
because of this data is search.

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:38.970
So in a typical search
algorithm that's using TF-IDF,

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:43.169
we look at the titles, the
tags, the descriptions,

00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:44.590
things like that.

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:47.520
But the challenge is that those
sellers, like Dianne Nordt,

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:50.310
can describe the wool blankets
any way that she wants.

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:52.590
She can talk in her
description about the sheep

00:08:52.590 --> 00:08:54.810
and how she raised them.

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:57.450
But someone else who's
making another wool blanket

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:59.335
might call it something
very different.

00:08:59.335 --> 00:09:01.767
And so that makes it
really, really challenging

00:09:01.767 --> 00:09:04.350
for a typical search-- you know,
solar index or something like

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:04.980
that--

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:06.910
to find the right results.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:08.820
And so this is one
of the areas that we

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:12.600
found that we can apply machine
learning onto to provide

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:14.530
better results for the sellers.

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:17.220
And so enter something
that we call CSR, which

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:19.950
is Context Specific Ranking.

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:22.230
So Etsy generates
almost a billion,

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:25.482
over a billion events
a day for their buyers.

00:09:25.482 --> 00:09:27.690
So the buyers are clicking,
they're looking at staff,

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:29.460
they favorite in,
they edit their cart,

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:30.900
they're purchasing things.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.100
All of that data gets processed.

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.060
And we can use that in our
machine learning algorithms

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:39.210
to re-rank the search results
so that the results end up

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:42.080
being exactly what the
buyers are looking for

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:43.620
and not a combination
of everything

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:46.530
that's possible out there.

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:48.410
So this is an example of that.

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:50.970
On the left, you'll see
a typical search result

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:53.706
for alarm clocks, in this case.

00:09:53.706 --> 00:09:56.340
But on Etsy, when you
search for alarm clocks,

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:58.950
you might be looking for
an actual alarm clock.

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:02.310
You also might be looking
for parts to an alarm clock

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:05.040
that you want to put in a piece
of art that you're making.

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:07.290
Or you might be looking for
stickers of alarm clocks.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:08.700
You might be looking for prints.

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:11.870
There's no telling what
you are really looking for.

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:13.080
And so enter CSR--

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:16.290
on the right-hand side, where
we've taken all of the queries

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:18.460
that happened days
and days before

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:20.820
and looked at what the
users interacted with.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:24.030
And we apply that back and
re-rank the search results.

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:28.350
And with that, we have something
that the user finds as a better

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:31.590
search result. And we see this
in the data of conversions,

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:34.860
the number of pages they have to
process through on their search

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:35.850
and all of this.

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:38.100
And all of that leads
us to show that it's

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.290
a better result for the buyers.

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:43.330
Another example
is wedding gowns.

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:45.720
On Etsy, you may be
looking for the material

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:49.184
to actually sew or
make your wedding gown.

00:10:49.184 --> 00:10:51.600
You can also be looking for
bridesmaids' gifts, or wedding

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:53.850
gown hangers, or any
other number of items

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:55.720
when you search
for wedding gowns.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:59.010
And so with CSR, we can
actually apply machine learning

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:02.850
on top of those search results
and produce a better result

00:11:02.850 --> 00:11:05.820
for the buyer.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:09.000
But all of this, as I
mentioned, takes a lot of data

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:12.880
and a lot of compute capacity.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:16.020
In fact, that 1.2
billion results,

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:19.950
sometimes it can take almost
24 hours to process that data.

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:23.850
And if we train our algorithms
on more than a couple

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:27.376
of weeks worth of data, we
run into capacity issues.

00:11:27.376 --> 00:11:30.520
And so if you're in
a traditional data

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:34.200
center, which we have
been, you know the cycle.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.479
You have to plan a budget
months and months ahead of time.

00:11:37.479 --> 00:11:38.895
And then you have
to order staff--

00:11:38.895 --> 00:11:41.020
depending if you're doing
large orders-- maybe even

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:42.200
a number of months ahead.

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:45.030
And then you need to receive
it, you need to rack it,

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:46.110
you need to set it up.

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:49.080
And finally, you can actually
let the developers have access

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:49.760
to it.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:52.230
And so this cycle is
really not conducive.

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:55.560
If you want to do fast
and really flexible

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:58.530
changes in the machine learning,
the data, the feature set,

00:11:58.530 --> 00:12:00.105
the feature
engineering, all of that

00:12:00.105 --> 00:12:03.510
that we want to have a
really high cycle time on

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:07.080
isn't very conducive.

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:11.400
But we also want to use
this in a sustainable way.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:14.940
If we didn't "keep commerce
human" in everything we do--

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:18.270
meaning the way we do our
compute power to the way

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:21.990
that we consume food and
energy in our offices,

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:23.730
the way we heat and
cool our offices.

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:27.030
If we didn't do it
in a sustainable way,

00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:30.690
it wouldn't be the way that
we're keeping commerce human.

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:33.695
And so sustainability is
a really big deal for us.

00:12:33.695 --> 00:12:35.880
And so we've got
to look at not only

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:39.690
how do we get a lot more
compute capacity very flexibly,

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:42.490
but in a sustainable fashion.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:44.610
So this graph in the upper
left is a typical sort

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:46.230
of e-commerce cycle.

00:12:46.230 --> 00:12:50.070
Even though we're global, the US
is one of our largest markets.

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:52.980
And this is the typical cycle
of the early evening hours.

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:56.100
You know, traffic isn't
as great, it spikes up.

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:58.230
You see the dip when everyone
sort of travels home,

00:12:58.230 --> 00:12:58.980
commutes home.

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:01.110
And then while they're
watching TV at night,

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:03.120
they're shopping on Etsy.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:07.400
Over on the right is the
idle time of our web servers.

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:09.180
And it's about inverse of this.

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:11.040
So when the site
gets very busy, we

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:13.970
start to reduce the CPU idle.

00:13:13.970 --> 00:13:18.834
When the site isn't so busy,
we get upwards of 90% idle.

00:13:18.834 --> 00:13:20.250
Now, the interesting
thing is this

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.770
is our energy consumption
in our data center.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:24.720
And you'll notice
that it's flat.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:28.479
Because we don't have a virtual
environment where we can share

00:13:28.479 --> 00:13:30.770
with someone else, we can't--
when we're not using it--

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:31.457
give it back.

00:13:31.457 --> 00:13:32.790
We don't shut down the servers--

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:33.956
once they're in, they're in.

00:13:33.956 --> 00:13:37.040
And so this isn't a
very sustainable way

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:41.890
to provide compute capacity
for the Marketplace.

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:43.530
And we care about
this so much, we

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:46.110
have publicly stated
our goals around having

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:47.340
ecological impact.

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:50.130
That we want a 100%
renewable energy

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:52.440
in the offices, and in the
data centers, and everything

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.200
that are on our compute by 2020.

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:59.220
And we want to reduce the
intensity of this by 25%

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:00.590
by 2025.

00:14:00.590 --> 00:14:02.820
And the majority of
that is our compute.

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:04.350
It's taken up by
the data center.

00:14:04.350 --> 00:14:08.340
And so that is a very big
goal of the engineering team,

00:14:08.340 --> 00:14:09.880
that is to focus on that.

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:13.200
And of course, we have other
ecological impact goals

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:15.750
that we've announced.

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:17.280
And so enter the cloud.

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:20.460
The cloud allows
us the flexibility

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:23.340
to keep power that
we need, all of this,

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:25.150
to do it in a sustainable way.

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:29.340
And so that's what started us
on the journey to look at cloud.

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:31.980
The other thing is we mentioned
we're a global company.

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:36.740
And as you know, performance
matters on e-commerce sites,

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:39.180
on search engines, all of this.

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:43.740
What you see is page load times
across different countries.

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:46.950
We've highlighted a couple
here for Brazil and India.

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:48.390
And you'll see
that they spike up

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:51.960
and have really, really
terrible load times.

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:54.600
Google's own research
shows that just increasing

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:56.850
from one to three
seconds, and you get

00:14:56.850 --> 00:14:59.380
a 32% increase in bounce rate.

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:01.260
And if you increase
it over 10 seconds,

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:03.900
you get 130-some
percent bounce rate.

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:05.700
And so this is really
important to us.

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:08.100
That when we're trying to
allow sellers and buyers

00:15:08.100 --> 00:15:10.110
across the world to
connect to each other,

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:12.240
we have to have really
fast performance.

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:16.140
And having our data
centers located in the US

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:17.340
doesn't help us with this.

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:20.910
Even though we use CDNs
for our static content,

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:22.620
we have a lot of
dynamic content that we

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:27.430
need to generate in the
data centers or on site.

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:31.230
And so having a global presence
with a cloud provider really

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:34.765
is going to enable us to
improve our performance.

00:15:34.765 --> 00:15:36.390
And so the decision
was made that we're

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:40.250
going to look for a
cloud provider partner.

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:43.230
But we really wanted one
that fit with our culture.

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:45.900
As I mentioned, Etsy has a
really strong engineering

00:15:45.900 --> 00:15:46.737
culture.

00:15:46.737 --> 00:15:48.570
And we wanted one that
we could partner with

00:15:48.570 --> 00:15:52.300
and really believe that
they get our culture.

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:54.390
We wanted one that focused
on machine learning.

00:15:54.390 --> 00:15:58.324
I describe the Marketplace
really as an iceberg.

00:15:58.324 --> 00:16:00.240
And the Marketplace is
the tip of the iceberg.

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:02.100
And behind it,
what you don't see,

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:04.110
is all of those
billion events that

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:07.800
get processed every single day
in our big data infrastructure.

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:09.880
And then all of that
machine learning--

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:12.240
I mentioned one small
application of it,

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:14.307
but we're applying
it in lots of ways.

00:16:14.307 --> 00:16:16.390
And we're going to keep
applying it more and more.

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:19.050
And so we wanted someone who
had a real focus on machine

00:16:19.050 --> 00:16:20.306
learning.

00:16:20.306 --> 00:16:21.930
We also wanted those
international data

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:24.180
centers so that we can
have points of presence

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:25.629
around the world.

00:16:25.629 --> 00:16:27.420
And then, of course,
that strong commitment

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:29.550
to environmental
sustainability--

00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:32.430
we wanted someone who really
understood what we meant

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:36.600
and how we felt about it,
and could partner with that.

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:39.494
So the question was,
well, how do you decide

00:16:39.494 --> 00:16:40.785
who fits all of these criteria?

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:48.480
So we took one big project
of migrate to the cloud

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:52.560
and broke it down into a
bunch of smaller projects.

00:16:52.560 --> 00:16:55.530
And we have-- even though
it's a single Marketplace--

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:59.327
we have hundreds of systems
within the Marketplace

00:16:59.327 --> 00:17:01.410
and within the big data,
and the machine learning,

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:04.230
and all of these
that power that.

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:07.140
And so we broke that into
all of these subprojects.

00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:11.160
We used a RACI model to
assign ownership of those.

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:15.089
And we asked those owners to
take that vision of that system

00:17:15.089 --> 00:17:17.220
through an architectural review.

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:19.560
Some of these systems are
going to be imported, lifted

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:20.099
and shifted.

00:17:20.099 --> 00:17:23.339
Some of them are going
to use new technologies.

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:25.859
Some of them are going to
be redesigned completely

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:27.179
to be cloud ready.

00:17:27.179 --> 00:17:29.220
And so we wanted the
owners of these systems

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:32.010
to take that through the
architectural review process.

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:35.640
And what came out of that is a
set of requirements, thousands

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:38.310
of requirements from all of
these subprojects of what

00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:40.590
they would need
going to the cloud

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:43.340
and out of the data center.

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:47.040
And so we took all of those and
used a tool called the decision

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:48.240
matrix.

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:50.529
We put all the requirements
into decision matrix.

00:17:50.529 --> 00:17:52.320
And then we worked with
our cloud providers

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:55.760
to do experiments, and to do
lots of technical meetings

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:58.440
and architectural reviews with
the different cloud providers

00:17:58.440 --> 00:18:01.440
to get their input on how
best to design and implement

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:03.270
our systems.

00:18:03.270 --> 00:18:07.020
And this decision matrix
looks something like this.

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:10.870
Across the top, we have our
functional requirements.

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:12.990
These are the things
that are overarchingly

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:15.280
important to our business--

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:18.180
the relationship, the
cost, the ease of use,

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:20.490
the value-added services,
security, locations.

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:23.230
All of these are things that
are really, really important.

00:18:23.230 --> 00:18:25.235
And they're kind of
overarching requirements.

00:18:25.235 --> 00:18:26.610
And then down on
the left, you'll

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:29.679
see all of these
subproject requirements

00:18:29.679 --> 00:18:30.720
that people came up with.

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:32.053
And there's literally thousands.

00:18:32.053 --> 00:18:35.340
This is just a
screenshot of that.

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:36.660
And then we weighted them.

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:39.990
And we said, these
customer requirements,

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:43.550
how did they impact the
functional requirements?

00:18:43.550 --> 00:18:46.857
And what we got out
of that was thousands

00:18:46.857 --> 00:18:48.690
of customer requirements
that were weighted.

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:53.170
So we knew which ones were
the most important to us.

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:55.740
We then used these
weighted scores

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:59.160
to have the engineers rank
the different providers based

00:18:59.160 --> 00:18:59.885
on that.

00:18:59.885 --> 00:19:02.190
And so as they went through
their experimentation

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:05.220
or their discussions, they
could use the decision matrix

00:19:05.220 --> 00:19:06.370
to rank them.

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:09.030
And they could say, this
provider fulfills those,

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:10.130
this provider doesn't.

00:19:10.130 --> 00:19:12.930
At the end, we cross-multiply
everything out.

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:15.090
You have tens of
thousands of data points

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:17.990
that you can compare
these providers with.

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:22.080
And in our case, GCP
had over 50,000 points.

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:23.786
They won by over 10%.

00:19:23.786 --> 00:19:26.640
And by using this
matrix, we tried

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:28.380
to eliminate people's bias.

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:31.870
And bias because they either
prefer one provider or another,

00:19:31.870 --> 00:19:35.550
or they only had experience
with one provider or another.

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:37.560
And we tried to
eliminate all of that

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:40.320
and make this a very
quantifiable approach

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:42.640
that we felt good with,
that we knew that they

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:44.790
were going to support
our requirements

00:19:44.790 --> 00:19:48.084
and meet our business needs.

00:19:48.084 --> 00:19:52.500
And so we're moving
very, very quickly.

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:55.440
We announced the
partnership in December

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:57.930
that we are going to migrate
everything out of our data

00:19:57.930 --> 00:19:59.850
centers to GCP.

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:02.790
We're on a two year timeline.

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:07.060
We almost immediately migrated
all of our image serving off

00:20:07.060 --> 00:20:10.050
of Etsy into GCP.

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:12.900
And lots of the lots of other
subprojects-- the machine

00:20:12.900 --> 00:20:16.170
learning, training,
infrastructure--

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:18.530
is now migrated to GCP.

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:21.850
And so lots of these
subprojects are already in step.

00:20:21.850 --> 00:20:24.630
But we're planning on a
full two year migration

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:27.690
to get all of our
systems out of there.

00:20:27.690 --> 00:20:28.870
Etsy Web is the big one.

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:30.640
And so we're trying
to break that down

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:33.840
to as many small
pieces as possible

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:37.974
to move the
Marketplace up to GCP.

00:20:37.974 --> 00:20:39.390
And we, of course,
have to do this

00:20:39.390 --> 00:20:43.200
around the busy times of year--
typical e-commerce company,

00:20:43.200 --> 00:20:48.617
where we have lots of traffic
in the Q4, around holidays.

00:20:48.617 --> 00:20:50.825
And so all of that has to
be taken into consideration

00:20:50.825 --> 00:20:52.650
in timing of this.

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:55.970
But we already have all of
this running in the cloud.

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:59.000
And we're doing load
or performance testing

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:00.325
on that right now.

00:21:00.325 --> 00:21:02.700
And so that's how we build
enough confidence is replaying

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:05.880
traffic against the
GCP infrastructure

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:07.870
with the Etsy Web
Marketplace running there.

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:13.346
And so all of that's going into
the migration step of this.

00:21:13.346 --> 00:21:18.050
And so just to recap before
we take some questions,

00:21:18.050 --> 00:21:20.590
we're a global marketplace.

00:21:20.590 --> 00:21:23.970
We really, really believe in our
mission of Keep Commerce Human.

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:27.660
And that means everything
that we do has to follow that.

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:30.210
And that means not only our
engineering, but the way

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:33.180
we host, the way we build
our products, all of this.

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:36.190
We have a very strong
engineering culture.

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:38.390
And we wanted someone,
a partner that

00:21:38.390 --> 00:21:44.310
could meet us there and
really fit within our culture.

00:21:44.310 --> 00:21:47.550
We made the decision and
are doing the migration

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:50.610
by dividing it up into a
number of small projects,

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:52.710
utilizing that decision
matrix so that we

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:55.830
have a quantifiable decision
that we can feel good about

00:21:55.830 --> 00:21:59.240
and stand behind that we
made the right decision.

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:01.110
And migrating to
GCP really allows

00:22:01.110 --> 00:22:04.110
us to do this in a sustainable
fashion, that they really

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:07.650
get where we're coming from in
terms of having 100% renewable

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:11.220
energy and sustainably.

00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:13.380
And really, we found,
I think, someone

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:14.990
that's proven to
be a great partner

00:22:14.990 --> 00:22:16.550
in the migration with us.

00:22:16.550 --> 00:22:19.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

